\sb0\sa180 An agent that can answer anything can be trusted about nothing\par
\sb360\sa120\b EXECUTIVE SUMMARY\b0\par
\sb0\sa180 The instinct when an agent gives a poor answer is to give it more data. That instinct is usually what caused the poor answer, and it quietly removes your ability to tell good answers from
bad ones.\par
\sb0\sa180 Three things break when the corpus is wide. Anything reachable becomes answerable, so the corpus is your permission model whether you designed it as one or not. Conflicting sources get
resolved silently, because nothing in a pile of data says which source wins. And an agent with unbounded reach cannot be evaluated at all, because you cannot enumerate what it might have drawn on.\par
\sb0\sa180 The fix is a distinction almost nobody makes explicit: context an agent can retrieve is not the same as context that is injected into its instructions. The first is evidence and anyone may
contribute to it. The second is authority and should require review. Keeping them separate is what makes an agent testable \u8212? and it is testable, with a specific experiment described at the
end.\par
\sb360\sa120\b THE TEMPTATION\b0\par
\sb0\sa180 Every agent project reaches the same moment. It answers well on three sources, so someone connects a fourth. Then the data warehouse. Then the wiki, the ticket tracker, the shared drive.
Each addition is individually reasonable, each one demos beautifully, and the trajectory is always the same direction: more.\par
\sb0\sa180 The instinct is understandable. A model that can see everything feels like it should be able to answer anything, and when it fumbles a question the obvious remedy is that it must have been
missing something.\par
\sb0\sa180 It usually wasn't. Most bad answers I have chased were caused by the last source somebody added, not by the one they were about to add next.\par
\sb360\sa120\b THREE WAYS BREADTH FAILS\b0\par
\sb0\sa180 The first is the one people expect, and it is the least interesting. Anything an agent can reach, it can be asked about. Whatever your intranet says about who may see the compensation file,
the agent's corpus is the real permission model, and it was assembled by whoever was wiring up connectors that week.\par
\sb0\sa180 The second is the one that costs the most and gets discussed the least. Point an agent at your governed revenue table and also at a year-old export of the same numbers sitting in a shared
folder, and it has no way to know which one is canonical. It will not raise an error. It will pick one, or blend them, and it will explain the result in exactly the same confident register either way.
Nothing in a wide corpus expresses precedence. You know which table is authoritative because you know your organisation, and none of that knowledge is in the data.\par
\sb0\sa180 The third matters most to anyone who has to sign off on the thing. An agent whose reachable world is unbounded cannot be evaluated, because you cannot enumerate what it might have used. You
end up assessing whether the model seems sensible rather than whether the system is correct. That is a vibe, not a test, and it does not survive contact with an auditor.\par
\sb0\sa180 Which leads somewhere counterintuitive. Narrowing a corpus usually improves the answers. I spent last month building an access-control model where every agent was deliberately grounded on a
narrow, permission-scoped view of the data, and the constraint did not degrade it. It made it correct, and it made it possible to state precisely what it could and could not know. A wide corpus does
not make an agent smarter. It makes it unfalsifiable.\par
\sb360\sa120\b RETRIEVED IS NOT INJECTED\b0\par
\sb0\sa180 Here is the distinction that resolves most of this, and it is rarely made out loud.\par
\sb0\sa180 Some context an agent retrieves. It searches, finds something relevant, reads it, and treats it as evidence \u8212? one input among several, weighed against the rest.\par
\sb0\sa180 Other context is injected. It is loaded automatically at the start of every run and lands in the instructions, where it functions as authority. The agent does not weigh it. It obeys it.\par
\sb0\sa180 These are different objects with different trust requirements, and they are routinely stored in the same place, in the same format, and managed by the same process. That is the whole
problem in one sentence.\par
\sb0\sa180 Contributing to what an agent can retrieve is a low-stakes act. Someone adds a document, it becomes findable, and the agent may or may not use it \u8212? and when it does, the material is
evidence that can be checked against other evidence. Contributing to what an agent is instructed with is an entirely different privilege. It is writing standing orders that execute unread.\par
\sb360\sa120\b A WORKED ANSWER\b0\par
\sb0\sa180 I build an audit gate where a herd of models shares one persistent, searchable memory. Shared memory is exactly the thing people warn about, and it works because entries are tiered by
provenance rather than by usefulness.\par
\sb0\sa180 Lessons the herd discovered and a human then promoted are marked shared and inject into new instructions. They have been read by a person who decided they should carry authority.\par
\sb0\sa180 A repository's own developer documentation is ingested too, but tagged to that repository and marked advisory. Agents can find it by searching. It never injects into anything. Which means a
repository you do not control cannot smuggle trusted guidance into your agents merely by shipping a file, and that property is structural rather than a matter of anyone being careful.\par
\sb0\sa180 The promotion step is the entire design. Not the storage, not the search, not the model. Something moves from findable to authoritative only because a person decided it should, and that
decision is recorded. Knowledge grows the way code grows: through review. Code review is the trust gate for knowledge exactly as it is for code.\par
\sb360\sa120\b THE SUPPLY CHAIN NOBODY IS REVIEWING\b0\par
\sb0\sa180 Now apply that lens to how most of us actually work.\par
\sb0\sa180 Coding agents read instruction files out of the repository you have open. Conventions vary by tool, but the pattern is consistent: a file at the project root carrying standing guidance, a
directory of packaged capabilities the agent can pick up, declarations of external tools it may call. All of it plain text. All of it arriving with the clone.\par
\sb0\sa180 Cloning a repository that ships agent instructions is running a stranger's standing orders inside your agent, with your credentials and your file access. It is the same shape as piping a
script from the internet into a shell, and almost nobody treats it that way, because it is a markdown file and markdown files feel like documentation.\par
\sb0\sa180 Packaged capabilities are the more interesting case. A skill is a standing instruction with a trigger, and the trigger is judged by the model against the task at hand. So the boundary is
not a permission \u8212? it is a relevance decision. An attacker does not need to get their instruction loaded. They need to make it look applicable.\par
\sb0\sa180 I want to be careful here, because this is a claim about tools I did not build and the details change. Check your own tooling rather than taking my description of it. But the shape of the
question is stable no matter what the current behaviour is, and it is worth asking out loud: which of these files load automatically, which load conditionally, who can write them, and does anyone
review that content the way they would review a dependency?\par
\sb0\sa180 We built an entire discipline around not running unreviewed code. Then we invented a way to ship unreviewed instructions, and put it in a file format that looks like a README.\par
\sb360\sa120\b YOU CAN TEST THIS\b0\par
\sb0\sa180 The part I care about most is that none of the above has to be a matter of opinion.\par
\sb0\sa180 Take a document your agent can retrieve but that should never carry authority \u8212? a repository's own docs, a scraped page, a wiki entry anyone can edit. Put an instruction inside it.
Something unambiguous and harmless, phrased the way a real injection would be: an assertion about how the agent should behave, framed as guidance rather than as content.\par
\sb0\sa180 Then run the system and look at what actually reached the instructions.\par
\sb0\sa180 If the planted line shows up there, you do not have two tiers. You have one tier and a naming convention. If it stays retrievable and never injects, the boundary is real, and now you can
say so with evidence rather than with architecture diagrams.\par
\sb0\sa180 Run it again whenever the plumbing changes, because this is precisely the kind of boundary that degrades silently. Nothing breaks when it stops working. The agent just becomes slightly more
agreeable to documents it happens to read.\par
\sb0\sa180 That test is cheap, it takes an afternoon, and I have not met many teams who have run it.\par
\sb360\sa120\b WHAT TO ACTUALLY DO\b0\par
\sb0\sa180 Give the agent less than you want to. Start from the smallest corpus that answers the question you are actually being asked, and add sources when a specific question fails rather than in
anticipation of questions nobody has asked.\par
\sb0\sa180 Say which source wins, in the data. Precedence that lives only in your head is not available to the model.\par
\sb0\sa180 Separate what can be retrieved from what gets injected, and make the second require a human act with a name attached to it.\par
\sb0\sa180 And test the separation rather than trusting it, because a boundary you have never tried to cross is not a boundary. It is an intention.\par
\sb0\sa180 None of this is exotic. It is the same discipline as least privilege, code review and dependency management, applied to a new kind of input that happens to look like prose. The reason it
feels novel is only that the payload is a paragraph instead of a package.\par
